Multimodal neural networks (MMNNs) feature multi-branch topologies that offer opportunities for inference parallelism. However, mainstream machine learning compilers prioritize intra-operator parallelism as the primary optimization target. While effective for chain-structured models, this approach inhibits the inter-operator parallelism inherent in the MMNNs, leading to suboptimal inference efficiency. Through a systematic analysis, we identify two fundamental sources of inefficiency: (1) during graph scheduling, a compromise among synchronization overhead, resource contention, and GPU utilization; and (2) during compilation, a structural conflict between greedily maximizing intra-operator parallelism and the severe resource competition induced by concurrent operators. This paper introduces HOPO, a compiler for accelerating MMNN inference via holistic parallelism optimization. HOPO integrates two key mechanisms: (i) a topology-aware graph scheduler that identifies scheduling granularity and operator combinations to balance GPU utilization, contention, and synchronization costs; and (ii) an auto-tuner that co-optimizes kernels for concurrently executed operators, enabling holistic parallelism rather than greedy, per-operator optimization. Extensive evaluation on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 across seven representative MMNN workloads demonstrates that HOPO achieves 1.50 × average speedup (up to 1.99 ×) over the sequential TVM baseline and 1.30 × average speedup (up to 1.48 ×) over the multi-stream TVM baseline.